<section class="tiny-results" aria-labelledby="reading-the-results">
  <header>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Objective, uncertainty & evidence</p>
      <h2 id="reading-the-results">What does a lower loss tell us?</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      The script evaluates held-out loss every 500 steps, on up to 50 batches.
      Cross-entropy and perplexity assess prediction; entropy describes
      confidence. All logarithms below are natural.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="metric-grid">
    <article>
      <h3>Cross-entropy</h3>
      <div
        class="metric-formula"
        aria-label="L equals minus the mean log probability assigned to the actual next token"
      >
        ℒ = −mean<sub>t</sub> log p(x<sub>t+1</sub> | x<sub>≤t</sub>)
      </div>
      <p>
        Penalizes low probability on the <strong>actual next token</strong>.
        This is the training objective, computed from raw logits.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Perplexity</h3>
      <div class="metric-formula">PPL = exp(ℒ)</div>
      <p>
        A different scale for the same loss. Compare runs using the{' '}
        <strong>same tokenizer and evaluation data</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Predictive entropy</h3>
      <div class="metric-formula">
        H(p) = −∑<sub>v</sub> p(v) log p(v)
      </div>
      <p>
        Measures how spread out the predictions are,{' '}
        <strong>not whether they are correct</strong>. Not logged in this
        homework.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <div class="experiment-grid">
    <figure class="loss-figure">
      <a
        href="/media/tinystories/training-loss.png"
        aria-label="Enlarge the original loss curve from the homework"
      >
        <img
          src="/media/tinystories/training-loss.png"
          width="1000"
          height="600"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Original training and validation cross-entropy curves fall from above 250 over about 300,000 gradient steps. The raster figure provides no exact final score."
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Original homework figure, p. 2. No final score is inferred from the
        raster image.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="result-findings">
      <div class="uniform-reference">
        <span class="eyebrow">Uniform prediction · 3,000 tokens</span>
        <strong>ℒ = ln(3,000) ≈ 8.01</strong>
        <span>PPL = 3,000 · H = 8.01 nats</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Loss falls, but the starting scale needs checking.</strong> The
        initial value exceeds 250, far above the uniform reference. Logit scale
        and initialization need inspection; a decline alone does not validate
        the model.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Generation remains incoherent.</strong> A recorded continuation
        of “One day” includes <q>al ummy yal yal yal</q>. Lower loss did not
        establish grammatical or narrative quality.
      </p>
      <p class="evidence-note">
        The separate perplexity curve uses example values in its plotting
        script; no measured final perplexity is claimed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <details class="entropy-note">
    <summary>Why low entropy can still mean a bad prediction</summary>
    <a
      href="/media/tinystories/cross-entropy.svg"
      aria-label="Enlarge the comparison of entropy and cross-entropy"
    >
      <picture>
        <source
          media="(max-width: 700px)"
          srcset="/media/tinystories/cross-entropy-mobile.svg"
        />
        <img
          src="/media/tinystories/cross-entropy.svg"
          width="700"
          height="230"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Toy four-token example. Entropy is 0.435 nats for both predictions; cross-entropy is 0.105 for a correct confident prediction and 3.401 for a wrong confident prediction."
        />
      </picture>
    </a>
    <p>
      Both distributions are equally concentrated. Only cross-entropy checks
      which token was actually correct.
    </p>
  </details>
</section>

